{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact head aa1425112440a9da1fd5c9d1fbe7b5660c5c9781, against the four candidates standing at that commit: 0d5c10c0 at internal/app, 2eb860ea at cmd/gs, 49aff5d6 at AGENTS.md, b6bbafc1 at SKILL.md. All four are effective, live and non-stale. I resolved them by commit rather than by sequence: the numbers in my instructions named #5361 to #5364, and the board had moved, so these four sit at #5373 to #5376. Anyone re-deriving them should key on the commit. I took no authority from old request #5259, head 25543905, its four artifacts, report #5276, review #5277 or approval #5283.\n\nCAVEAT, STATED VERBATIM SO THE MERGER CANNOT MISS IT: the conditions say architecture reanchor request 476b5f93 must land first, and this review is not merge authority unless the verdict and all four candidates remain non-stale after that landing.\n\nREBASE IDENTITY. The re-cut is a pure rebase and I proved it rather than assuming it. Comparing each head against its own merge base — e677dd8f for 25543905, b2dc4244 for aa142511 — the two branch deltas are both 195 lines, and every single difference between them is a hunk header: ten differing lines, zero of which are added or removed content. The guard body at this head is byte-identical to the one I approved. So my round-one substantive findings still describe this code, and I re-ran the load-bearing checks at this head rather than carrying them over on trust.\n\nRE-VERIFIED HERE. The guard sits in validateReportBasis, called from BuildActRequest, and refuses before anything is signed: the test asserts head and depth are unchanged after the refusal. Caller enumeration at this head, by grep rather than memory: cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:879, cmd/gitseq-github/main.go:454, cmd/gs/main.go:1405, and Workspace.Act at internal/app/app.go:714, which is what gs state and gs batch use. Every write path crosses it. Mutation both directions at this head: replacing the guard body with a discard fails TestApprovedReportMustRestOnItsNamedArtifactBeforeSigning with \"an approved report was signed without its named artifact basis\"; restored, it passes. Multi-artifact compatibility is proved by this verdict existing — it rests on four artifacts and names one, and was admitted. Defense in depth holds: validateMerge still refuses at cmd/gs/main.go:887.\n\nCHECKPOINT COEXISTENCE, the new axis. No interaction. The re-cut carries substantial checkpoint work — internal/kernel/checkpoint.go alone gains 153 lines — but internal/kernel/checkpoint.go names none of validateReportBasis, BuildActRequest or KindReport, so it neither reaches the report admission path nor is reached by it. The guard is an application-layer admission rule and the checkpoint work is kernel-layer sequence integrity; they share no call and no state. The full suite passing with both present is the positive side of the same check.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers are internal/app, the application host and admission boundary, and the two policy documents; cmd/gs changes only a test. architecture.md already describes internal/app as the layer applying application admission, so this adds a rule inside that contract rather than changing it, and needs no architecture.md edit or candidate. Kernel, storage, nexus and projection boundaries are untouched by the guard, and the checkpoint work beside it belongs to a different request.\n\nSECURITY. The trust boundary is approval authority: a ratified approval authorizes a merge and bounds what that merge may retire, so a malformed approval is worth refusing early. Pre-sign timing is the property that matters and it is asserted, not assumed — the refused approval leaves the workroom head and depth exactly where they were, so nothing is signed, submitted or appended. This is defense in depth and not a replacement, and both halves exist at this head. Two gaps I judge acceptable and record so nobody rediscovers them as surprises: a changes-requested verdict naming an artifact it does not rest on is not refused, which is harmless because only approvals are consumed as authority; and an approved report with an empty artifact field is left to validateMerge.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut. Nine lines in the function that already does this job, reusing a body it was already passed, no new surface or flag.\n\nDOCS. AGENTS.md and SKILL.md are byte-identical to the pair I checked in round one and each add one sentence. The prose is stricter than the guard rather than looser, since the guard refuses only an approved verdict naming a non-empty artifact, so it is advice that is always safe to follow and makes no claim of protection the code does not deliver.\n\nGATES, all five run by me fresh at this exact head, serialized by a ps check with no other runner. git diff --check clean, go vet ./... clean, go build ./... clean. go test ./... -count=1: 24 packages ok, internal/docset FAILED. go test ./internal/docset/... -count=1 alone: FAILED the same way. The failure is TestGateEveryNamedActResolvesToALiveRecord, reporting that 21ffa924 is retired with no successor so the pages naming it rest on a withdrawn pointer, with 20 citations known-failing. It is not this head. I checked out the merge base b2dc4244 and ran the docset gate there, and it fails identically on the same artifact, so the defect is pre-existing on the base and is a property of the durable log rather than of this diff — which no docs page or baseline entry in this branch delta touches. It is also, as far as I can tell, precisely what architecture reanchor 476b5f93 exists to repair, which is why the caveat above matters: if that landing does not clear this citation, the merger will still be looking at a red docset gate.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0d5c10c096c1f2d0d20a6e4d91d923f37f9343e7","head":"aa1425112440a9da1fd5c9d1fbe7b5660c5c9781","verdict":"approved"}}